--- body ---
## Context
Author ruling, 2026-10-27: raid, hunt, gaze and merge must need no forge. Split from ticket 1241
item 7 ("raid Phases 6-7 give a forge-less ready-to-merge branch a path through gating and merge,
or say raid needs a forge"): the second branch of that item is now excluded.

State on origin/main f52b159e: hunt already has a no-forge route (closes the ticket on the branch,
reviewer seats on `git diff <base>...HEAD`). gaze takes a PR number, and its setup and closing
scripts (`scripts/gaze_setup.py`, `scripts/gaze_close.py`, ticket 1227) read and write the PR
through `gh`. merge's recipe is `gh` throughout (each call behind `<!-- harness-extension-point -->`).
raid Phases 6-7 assume merge requests. Twelve helpers call `gh`, among them
`attribution_record.py`, `review_evidence.py`, `review-anchor.py`; `raid-sitrep.py` already has
`--no-forge`. The gate record itself (`merge-gate.py`, git notes) is already forge-free.

## Design questions (settle in Plan, advisor first, author if still open)
- What stands for the merge request: a branch plus a base ref, with the review result, attribution
  and gate verdict recorded in git (notes on the reviewed head, or `tickets/reviews/`)?
- What "merge" means with no forge: a local `git merge --no-ff` onto main guarded by the gate record,
  then a push if a remote exists; the main-update guard hook already accepts gated moves.
- Where each forge read/write gets its no-forge spelling: one adapter in the scripts, not a branch in
  every skill paragraph.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] gaze reviews a branch range with no forge and records its result where the gate reads it.
- [ ] verify-gate rules on that record with no forge.
- [ ] merge lands a gated branch onto main with no forge, with the same pinned-head and read-back
      guarantees as the forge recipe.
- [ ] raid Phases 6-7 run end to end on a repository with no remote forge; a test runs the
      gaze-gate-merge chain on a temp repo with `gh` absent from PATH.
- [ ] The forge path is unchanged (its tests pass); the harness gate passes.

## Relevant files
skills/gaze/, skills/merge/, skills/verify-gate/, skills/raid/SKILL.md, scripts/gaze_setup.py,
scripts/gaze_close.py, scripts/attribution_record.py, scripts/review_evidence.py,
scripts/review-anchor.py, scripts/merge-gate.py

Design note: conception/2026-10-27-git-notes-review-design.md (PR #1781, French). Read before
settling D1-D3. Two divergences to settle there: it splits review notes into four refs
(review-request, review-plan, review-report/, review-answer) where D2 has one refs/notes/review,
and it puts a four-environment transport trial (doudou, padme, ChatGPT Work, Claude Code Web)
before any harness migration, where wave 1 here starts with C1.

Monster check before Execute: likely a tracker with children (gaze, gate, merge, raid), each one
sign-off unit. Related: [[1241]], [[1227]], [[1248]].

## Raid annotation (design, 2026-10-27)
Monster check: yes. Blast radius ~20 files across 4 skills and 6 scripts, a symbol others depend on
(the adapter: 1255 and C2-C5 build on it), bundled sign-off units (gaze, gate, merge, raid), and a
hidden chain (gate reads what gaze writes; raid needs all three). Proposal: 1252 becomes the tracker.

Partition boundary: every forge read/write moves behind one module, `scripts/forge.py`; skills cite
operations, not `gh` spellings. Git-only state (merge-gate notes, worktrees, tier) stays where it is.

Up-front decisions (settle before C1; advisor first):
- D1 Merge-request stand-in: a review unit = (branch, base ref, head OID). `forge.resolve(ref)` takes a
  PR number (gh backend) or `branch:<name> [base=<ref>]` (none backend) and returns the same record:
  id, branch, base, head, state, body, close claims. No-forge close claims: `Ticket: tickets/<file>`
  commit trailers on base..head (hunt already closes on the branch, so this is mostly a cross-check).
- D2 Where results live: git notes on the reviewed head, same transport as merge-gate (push the ref when
  a remote exists, local otherwise): `refs/notes/review` carries the gaze result block and its
  `reviewer:`/`seat:`/`finding:` lines (attribution); verify-gate verdicts append there with
  `session=` and `round=`, so the round is derived from notes as it is from comments today. Not
  `tickets/reviews/`: a commit moves the head the result names. The gate record stays refs/notes/gate.
- D3 Local merge: in a throwaway detached worktree at the base tip, `git merge --no-ff <head-OID>`
  (pinned by OID, never by branch name), attribution block from D2 notes into the merge message,
  `merge-gate.py check` on the candidate, `update-ref refs/heads/<base> <new> <old>` (CAS = the pin),
  push the base if a remote exists, read back `rev-parse <base>^2 == head`, then sync-local-main.sh.
- D4 Adapter interface (`scripts/forge.py`, backends `gh` and `none`, chosen by `--forge auto|gh|none`
  / `IDH_FORGE`; auto = gh when gh is on PATH and the ref is a PR number): resolve, body,
  append_body_line, comment, results(marker), ready, auto_merge_armed, disarm, merge(unit, head),
  read_back(unit) -> (state, head, queued). Unsupported ops on `none` return a typed no-op, never raise.

Children (one sign-off unit each; Blocked-by = real prerequisite, never the tracker):
- C1 forge.py adapter: interface + gh and none backends, tests with gh absent from PATH and a stub gh.
  No caller moved. Blocked-by: none.
- C2 Move gaze_setup, gaze_close, attribution_record (append_to_pr), review-anchor onto the adapter;
  forge path byte-identical (compare outputs on a stub forge). Blocked-by: C1.
- C3 gaze takes `branch:<name>` with no forge, writes D2 notes; hunt's Review calls gaze on both routes
  (removes hunt's forge/no-forge divergence; answers 1241 item 9). Blocked-by: C2.
- C4 verify-gate reads D2 notes and writes its verdict/round there with no forge. Blocked-by: C3.
- C5 merge recipe no-forge spelling (D3) as the adapter's none merge/read_back, executed by
  raid_land.py. Blocked-by: C1, 1255.
- C6 raid Phases 6-7 forge-less + end-to-end test (temp repo, no remote, gh absent): gaze, gate,
  merge chain. Blocked-by: C4, C5.
Waves: 1 = C1 (+1256 in parallel, disjoint files); 2 = C2, 1255; 3 = C3, C5; 4 = C4; 5 = C6; then
the tracker's integration review. review_evidence.py `forge` subcommand stays gh-only (none = no
forge reviews, an empty admitted set, said so), out of scope.

## Advisor ruling (2026-10-27)
Advisor: Claude Fable 5.1 (one shot, no execution). Inputs: this body, the design note, merge-gate.py,
raid_land.py, gaze_setup.py, gaze_close.py, attribution_record.py, review-anchor.py, grep of `gh` in scripts/.
1. **One ref, `refs/notes/review` (D2 stands).** merge-gate.py already answers the note's §3.1 worry
   (several runs on one commit must not overwrite) with one ref + append-only entries chosen by latest
   (`append_and_push`, `parse_note`, `decide`); the same pattern carries a `kind:` field
   (request | plan | report | answer | verdict) plus `run=`/`seat=`/`session=`/`round=`. One ref = one
   refspec to fetch and push, which is the transport risk the note itself names; four refs (one per
   reviewer under review-report/) quadruple it and add a race per ref. refs/notes/gate stays apart
   (mechanical evidence, not reports). C3 writes request/plan/report/answer entries to that one ref
   through one helper (`review_notes.py` read/write, reusing merge-gate's fetch/append/push), never
   raw `git notes` in a skill; C4 appends `kind: verdict` entries there and derives the round from them.
2. **The transport trial gates nothing in 1252.** C1 and C2 touch no notes. C3/C4 write through the one
   helper, so a storage switch (branch-backed store, relay) if a web environment cannot push notes is a
   one-module change, not a rewrite. 1252's exit criteria name one repo with `gh` absent and no second
   environment: cross-environment review (note §6, objective 2) is a later ticket, and the trial gates
   that ticket. The doudou<->padme leg can run as a session-side probe beside C3; the ChatGPT Work and
   Claude Code Web legs need the author's accounts (point 4).
3. **C1 trimmed to the operations real call sites use** (D4 amended): `resolve(ref)` -> id, branch,
   base, head, state, mergeable, body, close claims (body is a field, not an op); `comments(ref)`
   (gaze_setup dumps comments+reviews; the marker filter is the caller's, so no `results(marker)`);
   `checks(ref)` (statusCheckRollup -> none|passing|failing); `comment`; `append_body_line` with
   read-back (attribution_record); `create`, `ready`, `auto_merge_armed`, `disarm`, `merge`,
   `read_back` (raid_land.GhForge, lifted as the gh backend). `none` backend in C1: resolve from
   `branch:<name> [base=<ref>]` by git only; comments/checks empty; comment/append_body_line/ready/
   disarm typed no-op; auto_merge_armed False; **merge and read_back raise a typed refusal until C5**:
   a silent no-op merge would let a caller read back "merged" on nothing. raid_land.py joins C2's list.
4. **Author's by right:** the web legs of the trial (their accounts and hours; not a 1252 blocker) and
   the rules/git.md wording once C5 lets a gated branch land with no forge (a rule edit, note §7); the
   ruling "forge stays the default route" bounds it. One-ref vs four is reversible: not the author's.
Children (1255, 1256 are closed; wave 1 is C1 alone): C1 forge.py, Blocked-by none. C2 gaze_setup,
gaze_close, attribution_record, review-anchor, raid_land onto the adapter, byte-identical forge path,
Blocked-by C1. C3 gaze on `branch:<name>` + review_notes.py helper + hunt on both routes, Blocked-by C2.
C4 verify-gate reads/writes verdict entries, Blocked-by C3. C5 none merge/read_back (D3) in raid_land,
Blocked-by C1. C6 raid 6-7 end to end, temp repo, gh absent, Blocked-by C4, C5. Cross-environment
review: separate ticket after the trial, not a child.
C1 exit criteria (from this ticket's own): the forge path is unchanged (its tests pass) and the harness
gate passes; the adapter's tests run with `gh` absent from PATH and with a stub `gh`; no caller moved.
**C1 can start now.**

